course: dictionary
lesson: 4479
title: Harshly: cruel or rough
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Harshly is an adverb. It tells you how something is done, like how someone was treated or how a voice sounded. Adverbs often end in -ly, and this one comes from the word harsh. «People often judge their own work more harshly than others do.» The word harshly tells you how people judge: too strictly, with few kind words.
explain: Ask yourself: what word would fit here? In the sentence about the emperor, «He punished harshly», the clues are forced labour and burned books. Those are cruel things, so harshly must mean in a cruel, unkind or too strict way. Try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Harshly has a second meaning too. It can describe something rough and unpleasant to the senses, like a light that is too bright and glaring, or a voice that sounds scratchy. Look at the words around it to find out which meaning fits.
fact: Harshly is an adverb, so it describes how an action is done.
fact: «Harshly» means in a cruel, unkind or too strict way, as in «treated harshly».
fact: «Harshly» can also mean in a rough, unpleasant way to the senses, as in «a light shining harshly».
fact: Useful phrases: speak harshly, treat harshly, judge harshly, punish harshly.
exercise: «People often judge their own work more harshly than others do.» What does harshly mean here? | *Too strictly, with few kind words | In a rough voice like a bell | Kindly and gently | Quickly and happily
exercise: «Qin Shi Huang punished harshly, using forced labour and burning books.» What does harshly tell us? | *In a cruel, unkind way | In a soft, gentle way | In a quiet, sleepy way | In a happy, playful way
exercise: Ella says the light in the room was shining harshly, and it hurt her eyes. What does harshly mean here? | Very slowly | *In a rough, unpleasant way to the senses | In a kind way | Loudly, like a shout
test: Which word could replace harshly in «The judge sentenced the thief harshly»? | *Severely | Gently | Happily | Quietly
test: «The wind blew harshly across the empty field.» What does harshly mean here? | Quickly and quietly | *In a rough, unpleasant way | Very happily | Gently and softly
exam: Grandad says, «Don't judge yourself too harshly, lad.» What does harshly mean? | *Too strictly and unkindly | Very quickly | Happily and loudly | Too kindly and softly
